{"duration_api_ms":163639,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0b241da4-39e0-425f-a006-4b009db579ae","total_cost_usd":0.23816360000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11133,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":19272,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":15211},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11133,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":19272,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11133,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11133},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":19272,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11133,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.23816360000000003,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":15211,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nVoyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began short-range observations of the planet on Jan. 31, 1987, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the nine previously known rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (450,000 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 559 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus' smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both documents use the identical base title.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft name Voyager 3 to Voyager 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Fixed 'Ur anus' typo and corrected Saturn to Neptune.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began short-range observations of the planet on Jan. 31, 1987, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected Voyager 1 to Voyager 2 and restructured sentence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected year 1968 to 1986 and swapped mislabeled km/miles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Identical title text; base document's title kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the nine previously known rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected moon count, spellings, naming allusion, date, and tilt figure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b3\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (450,000 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 559 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected unit conversion errors in speed and depth figures.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus' smaller moons.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected misspelled and misidentified moon names.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected 'century-long' to 'decade-long' for accuracy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected date and crew death count of Challenger disaster.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Only Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 spacecraft exist.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2's Uranus flyby geometry was set for its next target, Neptune.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began short-range observations of the planet on Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2, not Voyager 1, made the Uranus flyby.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 reached Uranus in 1986, and the km/mile labels were swapped.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons\",\n      \"corrects\": \"discovered 11 new moons\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 discovered ten new Uranian moons, matching the names listed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787\",\n      \"corrects\": \"obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Uranus's moons are named for Shakespeare characters, a tradition from 1787.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"two new rings in addition to the nine previously known rings\",\n      \"corrects\": \"three new rings in addition to the \\u201colder\\u201d eight rings\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Nine rings were known before Voyager 2, which found two more.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\n      \"corrects\": \"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Uranus's magnetic field is tilted about 59 degrees from its rotation axis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"450 km/h (450,000 meters per hour)\",\n      \"corrects\": \"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"450 km/h converts to 450,000 meters per hour, not 72,400.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"some 559 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\n      \"corrects\": \"some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"900 kilometers converts to about 559 miles, not 479.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus' smaller moons\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus' smaller moons\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Titan is a moon of Saturn; the Uranian moon is Titania.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"its nearly decade-long travels\",\n      \"corrects\": \"its nearly century-long travels\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, about nine years before the 1986 flyby.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986\",\n      \"corrects\": \"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Challenger disaster on Jan. 28, 1986, killed all seven crew members.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":139628,"type":"result","duration_ms":163760,"uuid":"ed97dcee-f7a5-4b44-9fd7-99f79a4245af","ttft_stream_ms":1650,"time_to_request_ms":122,"first_content_frame_ms":1650,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
